      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© The Author(s) 2015.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Cardiac disease is the leading cause of death in Lebanon, accounting for 22% to 26% of total deaths in the country. A thorough understanding of perceptions of cardiac illness and related self-care management is critical to the development of secondary prevention programs that are specific to the Lebanese culture."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "PURPOSE",
            "content": "To explore the cultural perceptions of cardiac illness and the associated meaning of self-care among Lebanese patients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Using a qualitative descriptive method, semistructured interviews were conducted with a purposive sample of 15 Lebanese cardiac patients recruited from a medical center in Beirut, Lebanon."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "FINDINGS",
            "content": "The qualitative descriptive analysis yielded one overarching and two other themes describing perceptions of cardiac illness and self-care within the Lebanese cultural context. The overarching cultural theme was, \"Lebanese cardiac patients were unfamiliar with the term concept and meaning of self-care.\" Lebanese cardiac patients thanked God and accepted their fate (Theme I). The participants considered their cardiac incident a life or death warning (Theme II)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "IMPLICATIONS FOR PRACTICE",
            "content": "Health care providers need to consider patients' cultural perception of illness while planning and evaluating cardiac self-care programs."
          }
        ]
      },
